# Anchored with a leading slash wherever the intent is "the one at the root".
#
# A bare `assets` in gitignore matches a directory of that name at EVERY level,
# not just this one — so it silently swallowed src/shared/assets/catalog.ts,
# src/main/assets/scan.ts and src/main/assets/place.ts. They were never
# committed, nothing said so, and the first sign of it was CI failing to
# typecheck a module that exists perfectly well on the machine that wrote it.

node_modules/
/out/
/dist/
.DS_Store
*.log

# Python sidecar
sidecar/.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# The asset library. Currently a symlink to the existing library while
# placement is undecided (docs/ASSETS.md §"Open decision"), so it must not be
# committed — but only THIS one, hence the slash.
/assets
/asset-catalog.json

*.tsbuildinfo

# Scratch left behind by tools handed ":memory:" as a database path. The colon
# makes the name illegal on Windows, and git cannot even check out a tree that
# contains one: `error: invalid path`, exit 128, before a single test runs.
*:memory:*

# The meme vault and the matted stickers — 2.7GB, and not ours.
#
# `Master_Viral_Meme_Vault` is the scraped originals; the `_COMPLETE` folder is
# the same set after background matting. They are source material for the
# sticker library, they are other people's footage, and together they are larger
# than every other thing in this repository by three orders of magnitude.
#
# Anchored, because the intent is these two at the root. Committing them once
# would be permanent: git history does not forget, and GitHub refuses a single
# file over 100MB. What the library NEEDS in the repo is the catalogue that
# describes them, never the footage itself — the same split /assets already
# makes.
/Master_Viral_Meme_Vault/
/Master_Viral_Meme_Stickers_COMPLETE*/

# Reference recordings — other people's work, kept for study.
#
# Instagram reels and app screen-recordings that features were measured
# against. They belong on this disk and not in a public repository: they are
# not ours to redistribute, and they are a hundred megabytes of video. What
# they TAUGHT us is written down in docs/REFERENCES.md, which is committed —
# so the findings survive even if the files do not.
/references/recordings/
